# mgnathan.msg - Nathan Vargas, patriot of a vanished country (mgnathan.ssl).
# 100-102 look, 110-126 floating remarks, 200+ dialogue. One entry per line; no braces in comments.

{100}{}{You see an old man in a waistcoat, sitting very straight beside a wrecked tanker truck.}
{101}{}{You see Nathan Vargas. He is listening to something you can't hear.}
{102}{}{His boots are polished, which in Megaton is a political statement. A dented radio sits on a crate beside him with its aerial pointed carefully at nothing.}

# 110-119: any day.
{110}{}{The President says prosperity is right around the corner.}
{111}{}{You mark me: America never left. It's only been quiet.}
{112}{}{Heard him again last night, clear as a bell. "My fellow Americans."}
{113}{}{Manya says turn it down. A man can't turn down his country.}
{114}{}{Any day now they'll come up that road with flags. Clean ones.}
{115}{}{Forty years hauling freight and I never once paid a tax. I feel bad about that now.}
{116}{}{The truck doesn't run and the radio does. I know which I'd rather.}
{117}{}{God bless the Enclave, wherever it's got to.}
{118}{}{Stand up straight, people. You never know who's watching.}
{119}{}{I've kept the anthem in my head sixty years. Most of it. The loud part.}

# 120-121: the bomb is disarmed.
{120}{}{I'll tell the President about that bomb when he gets here. He'll want to know.}
{121}{}{One of ours, that bomb. I always said it would come round.}

# 125-126: Simms is dead.
{125}{}{A lawman shot dead in a saloon. It wouldn't happen with a proper government.}
{126}{}{Somebody should lower a flag. Somebody should find a flag.}

# Greetings.
{200}{}{Hold still. Let me look at you. Good posture, clear eyes; you'd have made a fine citizen. Nathan Vargas, retired from the caravans, loyal to these United States. Have you heard the broadcasts?}
{201}{}{The patriot returns. He's on again tonight. I had a second chair for visitors; Manya burned it. Stand.}
{202}{}{The hero of Megaton! You're going in my letter to the President. I haven't got an address for him yet. You're going in it all the same.}
{203}{}{Lucas Simms, murdered in a saloon. That's what comes of making your own laws out of scrap. The real ones are on their way. You'll see.}
{204}{}{Speak up. The ears are sixty-five years old and the radio's had the best of them.}
{205}{}{Something else, Nathan.}

# Hub options.
{210}{}{What broadcasts?}
{211}{}{You worked the caravans?}
{212}{}{Who is Manya?}
{213}{}{Radio man talk to you?}
{214}{}{Keep listening, Nathan.}
{215}{}{Bye, radio man.}

# The broadcasts.
{220}{}{On the radio! Late, when the dust settles. A man who calls himself the President. He says the government never fell; it went away to plan, and it's coming back to put all this right. He has a wonderful voice.}
{221}{}{Put it right how?}
{222}{}{He doesn't say, exactly. "Purify." That's a word he likes. Make it all the way it was. I expect it means roads.}
{223}{}{There is no United States. Not for a century and a half.}
{224}{}{That's what Manya says. That's what they all say. And yet somebody is reading the news in a clean shirt; I can hear that it's clean. You don't get a voice like that out of a tent.}
{225}{}{Where does this President broadcast from?}
{226}{}{West. Out past the water, "from the last bastion," he says. I told Lucas Simms. He said anybody with that many guns and that much patience isn't coming to hand out seeds. Lucas has no vision.}

# The caravans.
{230}{}{Forty years. Klamath, the Den, as far south as there was road, and then some. I sold my maps to Moriarty for a bottle and a promise. I've still got the promise.}
{231}{}{What's out there?}
{232}{}{Klamath's honest: trappers and stink. The Den will sell you anything, and then sell you. Past that, ask Moriarty; he has my maps. All I have is my knees, and they remember every mile.}

# Manya.
{240}{}{My wife, forty-one years. She's in the cab. She is not receiving. She says I have the history of this town wrong and its future worse. She was here when the wall went up, so she thinks she knows.}
{241}{}{What does she say happened?}
{242}{}{That the bomb never dug this crater; the plane carrying it did. That her father talked folk into the wall, and the church people did the lifting so their bomb would stay put. She tells it plain. No flags in it.}

# Low Intelligence.
{250}{}{He talks to all of us, son. He's the President. You'd like him. He uses short words for the important parts.}
{251}{}{Me like President.}
